---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { dead } from "../lib/people.js";
import places from "../data/places.json";
import reads from "../data/scilla-reads.json";
const here = dead.filter((p) => [p.birthPlace, p.deathPlace, p.burialPlace]
  .some((x) => (x || "").startsWith("Scilla")));
const pl = places.find((x) => x.name.startsWith("Scilla")) || {};
const surnames = [...new Set(here.map((p) => p.surname).filter(Boolean))].sort();
---
<Base title="Scilla" eyebrow="Reggio Calabria"
      dek="The rock at the northern gate of the Strait of Messina, and the fishing quarter beneath it. Two of the four grandparents were born here, four years apart, and did not marry until Brisbane.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{here.length}</div><div class="l">people recorded at Scilla</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{pl.first || "1821"}</div><div class="l">the earliest birth named here</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">2</div><div class="l">of the four quarters begin here</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The place</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Scilla is the <em>Scylla</em> of the <em>Odyssey</em> — the rock opposite the whirlpool of
      Charybdis, at the narrowest point of the strait that separates Calabria from Sicily. The
      <strong>Castello Ruffo</strong> sits on the headland; below it, on the water, is
      <strong>Chianalea</strong>, a lane of houses built straight into the sea where the boats are
      moored at the doors. This was a swordfishing town, and it still is.</p>
      <p>The geography is the point for this family. From Chianalea you can see
      <a href={u("/piedimonte-etneo")}>Etna</a> on a clear morning. The Scilla families and the
      Piedimonte Etneo family were looking at each other across thirty kilometres of water for
      generations — and met on the other side of the world.</p>
      <p>Scilla was levelled by the <strong>1783 earthquake</strong> and the tsunami that followed it,
      when a wave took a large part of the population off the beach at Marina Grande. As at
      <a href={u("/briatico")}>Briatico</a>, that is why the record here begins where it does.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The families</h2>
    <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;line-height:2;margin-top:.8rem">
      {surnames.map((s, i) => <>{i > 0 && " · "}<strong>{s}</strong></>)}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The registers, opened 10 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">An index that names the parents — and misleads</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Scilla is <strong>not</strong> in FamilySearch's full-text corpus — four searches
      place-matched to it returned nothing, and its Calabrian holdings are Catanzaro and Vibo
      Valentia. But <strong>Antenati has 57 volumes</strong> from the Archivio di Stato di Reggio
      Calabria, births from <strong>1850</strong>; marriages run <strong>1905–1913 only</strong>.</p>
      <p>The pre-unification <em>Stato civile della restaurazione</em> index is laid out far better
      than the Italian one that replaced it. Its columns are <strong>N. d'ord · Cognomi e Nomi dei
      nati · Patria · Professione · NOMI E COGNOMI DEI GENITORI · giorno della nascita</strong>.
      <strong>The index names the parents</strong> — a whole year's households of one surname read off
      a single opening.</p>
      <p><strong>And it will mislead you if you trust it.</strong> See below.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 228 of 1861</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Rocco Arena, born 20 December 1861</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act228-arena-rocco.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1861, act 228" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">L'anno mille ottocento sessant<strong>uno</strong> il dì
        <strong>ventitre</strong> del mese di <strong>Dicembre</strong>… Comune di <strong>Scilla</strong>,
        Distretto di <strong>Reggio</strong>, Provincia della Prima Calabria Ulteriore, è comparso
        <strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong>, <strong>figli di Filippo</strong>, di anni
        <strong>quarantadue</strong>, di professione <strong>marinaro</strong>, domiciliato in Scilla…
        è nat[o] da <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong>, di anni <strong>ventotto</strong>… nel giorno
        <strong>venti</strong> del mese di Dicembre… il nome di <strong>Rocco</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p><strong>Three generations in one act:</strong> <strong>Filippo Arena</strong>, his son
        <strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong> — born about 1819, and a <em>marinaro</em>, a sailor —
        and the boy <strong>Rocco</strong>. Witnessed by Giuseppe Idone, 64, and Francesco Antonio
        Polì, 70, <em>proprietario</em>; the parish note records the baptism on the 23rd.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        della restaurazione, Scilla, Nati 1861, atto 228 — Antenati <code>an_ua2002933</code>, image 117.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 206 of 1883</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Giovanni Polistena, born 16 September 1883</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act206-polistena-giovanni.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1883, act 206" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">L'anno milleottocento ottant<strong>atre</strong>, addì
        <strong>diciassette</strong> di <strong>Settembre</strong>… Uffiziale dello Stato Civile del
        Comune di <strong>Scilla</strong>, è comparso <strong>Camillo Antonio Polistena</strong>, di
        anni <strong>quarantotto</strong>, <strong>pescatore</strong>, domiciliato in questo Comune…
        nella casa posta in <strong>Via Grotte</strong> al numero <strong>quarantasette</strong>, da
        <strong>Giovanna Romano</strong> sua moglie, <strong>filatrice</strong>, seco lui
        convivente… a cui dà il nome di <strong>Giovanni</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p><P n="Angela Polistena" />'s father, born at half past nine at night in a house on
        <strong>Via Grotte</strong> — the lane of houses standing in the water at
        <strong>Chianalea</strong>. His own father <strong>Camillo Antonio Polistena</strong> was
        forty-eight, so born about <strong>1835</strong>, and a <strong>fisherman</strong>.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem"><strong>The father agrees with the family tree</strong>, which
        calls him Antonio Polistena. <strong>The mother does not.</strong> The register says
        <strong>Giovanna Romano</strong>; the tree says <strong>Giovanna Pontillo</strong> — and gives
        that Giovanna two parents <em>both</em> surnamed Pontillo, which reads like an error in the
        tree rather than a second marriage.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        italiano, Scilla, Nati 1883, atto 206 — Antenati <code>an_ua16601246</code>, image 73.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">What the registers keep saying</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">They were sea families</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Every man the registers have named in this archive's Calabrian lines works the water:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
        <div class="k">Scilla, 1861</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong> — <em>marinaro</em></div><div class="w">sailor</div>
        <div class="k">Scilla, 1883</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Camillo Antonio Polistena</strong> — <em>pescatore</em></div><div class="w">fisherman</div>
        <div class="k">Briatico, 1886</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Alfonso Prostamo</strong> and his father <strong>Domenico</strong> — <em>marinari</em></div><div class="w">sailors</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>The family tree records not one of them as anything.</strong>
      Two provinces, three families, and the same trade — which is exactly what you would expect of
      Chianalea and the Costa degli Dei, and exactly what an undated pedigree cannot tell you.
      The Sicilian <a href={u("/piedimonte-etneo")}>Mazza</a>, up on the mountain, are
      <em>braccianti</em> and <em>contadini</em> to a man. <strong>The archive can now say what these
      people did, which it could not this morning.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">A correction, in the open</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The index said something else, and the caution caught it</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Reading the 1861 index, this archive recorded Rocco Arena's parents as
      <strong>«Giuseppe e Zirilli Grazia»</strong> — and noted that while the rare surname
      <em>Zirilli</em> agreed with the family tree's <em>Giovana Zirilli</em>, both forenames did not.
      It was filed as a <strong>candidate, not a join</strong>, with the words <em>"read the act
      itself before joining"</em>.</p>
      <p><strong>The act says Francesco Antonio Arena and Serafina Arlotta.</strong> The index's
      parents column had been misaligned by one row: it is a wide ruled table photographed across a
      gutter, and once the columns are cropped apart the row a name sits on is not reliably the row
      its parents sit on.</p>
      <p><strong>That caution is the only reason the archive does not now carry a wrong set of
      great-great-grandparents.</strong> Had the surname agreement been treated as sufficient — as
      the <a href={u("/register-1879")}>Venerio reading</a> was, earlier the same day — it would have
      been merged and published.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">And an unresolved conflict</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">This is not the tree's Rocco Arena</h2>
    <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
      <div class="k">The tree says</div>
      <div class="v">Rocco Arena, b. 1861, son of <strong>Orazio Arena</strong> and
      <strong>Giovana Zirilli</strong></div><div class="w">no source</div>
      <div class="k">The register says</div>
      <div class="v">Rocco Arena, b. <strong>20 December 1861</strong>, son of
      <strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong>, marinaro, and <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong></div>
      <div class="w">atto 228</div>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px">The 1861 Scilla register contains <strong>exactly one
    Rocco Arena</strong>, and <strong>neither parent matches</strong>. So either the tree's parents
    are wrong, or its birth year is. This archive has already found the same tree
    <strong>six years out</strong> on Salvatore Mazza's birth and six on Orazio Mazza's, so the year
    is the likelier error — but that is a hypothesis, not a finding, and the Arena line stays where it
    was until a record settles it. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>The queue →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Everyone the archive places here</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="m">Dates</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {here.sort((a, b) => (a.born || 9999) - (b.born || 9999)).map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={u("/people/" + p.slug)}>{p.name}</a></td>
              <td class="m">{[p.born, p.died].filter(Boolean).join("–")}</td>
              <td>{p.birthPlace}</td>
              <td>{p.deathPlace}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
